WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.576 --> 00:00:02.322
- [Narrator] When we
think about volcanoes,

00:00:02.322 --> 00:00:05.565
we imagine these eruptive,
hot, violent gateways

00:00:05.565 --> 00:00:07.595
to the center of the Earth.

00:00:07.595 --> 00:00:11.642
And I spoke to a scientist
who spends the night in them.

00:00:11.642 --> 00:00:14.304
- [Ken] My name is Ken
Sims, and I'm a professor

00:00:14.304 --> 00:00:17.944
of isotope geology at the
University of Wyoming.

00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:21.186
- [Narrator] Ken studies,
among other things, volcanoes.

00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:23.650
And it's for a few reasons,
but one in particular.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.358
- [Ken] You can start to forecast

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:27.581
the likelihood for another eruption.

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:29.113
- [Narrator] To get his research data,

00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:32.976
he has to spend time in
them, sometimes, overnight.

00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:35.426
So we're talking about what it feels like

00:00:35.426 --> 00:00:38.759
to spend the night in an active volcano.

00:00:40.656 --> 00:00:42.432
- [Ken] You're in a big crater.

00:00:42.432 --> 00:00:46.854
It's spectacular; this is like
being in a steep, deep canyon

00:00:46.854 --> 00:00:49.479
except for now it's circular
all the way around you.

00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:52.625
There's a lava lake below you
and the summit is above you.

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:55.046
- [Narrator] Volcanoes
have a particular smell

00:00:55.046 --> 00:00:57.883
because they're full of gas.

00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:01.344
- [Ken] Those gasses are
very sulfurous, so they smell

00:01:01.344 --> 00:01:04.370
strong and acrid, they
also have acids in 'em.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.006
And so they're toxic.

00:01:06.006 --> 00:01:08.800
- [Narrator] The air can
be dangerous to breathe in.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.522
- [Ken] You know, you oftentimes
are wearing gas masks;

00:01:10.522 --> 00:01:12.864
it's pretty hard to sleep with gas mask.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:15.349
- [Narrator] And it's noisy.

00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:16.719
- [Ken] It's echoey, typically,

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.780
you'll hear hissing.
- (metallic hissing)

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.718
- [Narrator] Instead of a campfire,

00:01:21.718 --> 00:01:23.626
there's an active lava lake.

00:01:23.626 --> 00:01:25.505
- [Ken] Looking at a lava lake is just

00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:28.147
phenomenal that way, it's mesmerizing.

00:01:28.147 --> 00:01:30.441
There's something about the

00:01:30.441 --> 00:01:33.246
red warm glow, it's unworldly.

00:01:33.246 --> 00:01:35.526
- [Narrator] There's a lot
to distract you from sleep.

00:01:35.526 --> 00:01:37.863
But Sims says none of that matters.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:40.196
(exploding)

00:01:41.804 --> 00:01:43.783
- [Ken] You don't wanna go to bed.

00:01:43.783 --> 00:01:45.889
This is how the Earth's surface formed

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:48.399
was from volcanoes, and
if you're in the moment,

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.240
it's amazing.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.573
- [Narrator] That's so cool.

